This C program for the Raspberry Pi Zero W uses the `libgpiod` library to control a common-anode RGB LED connected to GPIO pins 17 (red), 22 (green), and 27 (blue). It reads the CPU load every 500 milliseconds, maps the load to a color gradient, and sets the corresponding PWM values for the RGB LED. The program handles SIGINT and SIGTERM signals by turning off the LED and exiting cleanly.

The program initializes `libgpiod`, sets up signal handlers, and enters a loop where it reads the CPU load, maps it to a color, sets the PWM values for the LED, and sleeps for 500 milliseconds. If any error occurs during the read or setting of PWM values, it prints an error message and exits.

The `map_load_to_color` function maps the CPU load percentage to RGB values for the common-anode LED, where 0% is green, 50% is yellow, and 100% is red.

The `set_pwm_value` function sets the PWM value for a specified GPIO pin.

The `handle_signal` function turns off all GPIO pins used for the LED and cleans up before exiting the program.

The `setup_gpiod` and `cleanup_gpiod` functions initialize and uninitialize `libgpiod` respectively.

