        fprintf(logFile, "Timestamp: %s\nLeft Sensor: %d\nMiddle Sensor: %d\nRight Sensor: %d\nLeft Motor Speed: %d\nRight Motor Speed: %d\n\n", buffer, left_sensor, middle_sensor, right_sensor, left_motor_speed, right_motor_speed);

        // Wait for next sampling interval
        ts.tv_sec = rawtime + SAMPLING_INTERVAL_MS / 1000;
        ts.tv_nsec = (SAMPLING_INTERVAL_MS % 1000) * 1000000;
        nanosleep(&ts, NULL);
    }
}

int main() {
    setup_gpiod();
    signal(SIGINT, handle_SIGINT);

    logFile = fopen("trace_log.txt", "a+");
    if (!logFile) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to open log file: %s\n", strerror(errno));
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    pthread_t thread;
    if (pthread_create(&thread, NULL, &main_loop, NULL) != 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to create thread: %s\n", strerror(errno));
        cleanup();
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    // Wait for the thread to complete
    pthread_join(thread, NULL);

    cleanup();

    return 0;
}
```

This C program controls a line-following robot using a Keywish 3-Way Infrared Tracking Sensor and two DC motors driven through an L298N motor driver on a Raspberry Pi Zero W. It uses the `gpiod` library for GPIO operations and logs sensor readings and motor speeds to a file named `trace_log.txt` with ISO 8601 timestamps.

Key points:
- Initializes `gpiod` and requests GPIO pins.
- Sets up a SIGINT handler to stop motors and clean up.
- Reads sensors every 10 ms and adjusts motor speeds accordingly.
- Logs data to `trace_log.txt` with timestamps.
- Runs in a separate thread for continuous operation.

Ensure you have the `gpiod` library installed on your Raspberry Pi before compiling and running this program. You can install it using `sudo apt-get install libgpiod-dev` on Debian-based systems.

Compile the program with:
```bash
gcc -o line_follower line_follower.c -lgpiod
```

Run the compiled program:
```bash
./line_follower
```

To stop the program, send a SIGINT (usually `Ctrl+C`).

